#{[17592186425853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425853 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,3)=-9,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=x,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)=-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,3),$ estimate the value of $f(0.98,-0.01,3.1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7afc5f-975b-58b4-8d28-e717181d277e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425853 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,3)+∇f(1,0,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =-9+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,3),{f}_{y}(1,0,3),{f}_{z}(1,0,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,0,3)$, ${f}_{y}(1,0,3)$ and ${f}_{z}(1,0,3),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,0,3) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,0,3) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(1,0,3) & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.98,-0.01,3.1)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.98,y=-0.01$ and $z=3.1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.98,-0.01,3.1) & ≈-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨0.98-1,-0.01,3.1-3⟩ \\\\  & =-9+⟨0,1,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.02,-0.01,0.1⟩ \\\\  & =-9-0.01-0.6 \\\\  & =-9.61\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98327" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(3,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(3,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(3,-1)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,-0.97).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bbf719bd-56e4-5b5f-a665-42577dad1834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425854 #edb/ref 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(3,-1)+∇f(3,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(3,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(3,-1),{f}_{y}(3,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.01,-0.97)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01$ and $y=-0.97,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,-0.97) & ≈-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,-0.97+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =-3-0.01+0.03 \\\\  & =-2.98\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
